1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,482
A GraphQL query for a content type returns its items, and you decide

2
00:00:05,482 --> 00:00:08,268
which ones, in what order, and how many.

3
00:00:08,268 --> 00:00:12,993
In this video, you filter with where, sort with orderBy, page with first

4
00:00:12,993 --> 00:00:15,900
and skip, and choose versions with status.

5
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:26,476
Each content type that GraphQL exposes becomes a query field with the same five arguments.

6
00:00:26,476 --> 00:00:29,527
The content items field type declares them:

7
00:00:29,527 --> 00:00:35,690
where filters, orderBy sorts, first and skip page, and status picks the version of each item.

8
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:41,040
A query without arguments returns the published version of each item of the

9
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:44,731
type that the user may view, newest first, up to 100 items.

10
00:00:44,731 --> 00:00:47,401
You can change each default in the query.

11
00:00:47,401 --> 00:00:51,660
The default number of items can also be changed in the configuration.

12
00:00:52,410 --> 00:00:55,917
The field type builds one database query from them.

13
00:00:55,917 --> 00:01:01,270
The page is taken last, so first and skip count within the filtered, sorted list.

14
00:01:05,557 --> 00:01:07,807
This is GraphiQL, under Tools.

15
00:01:07,807 --> 00:01:12,371
The query asks every blog post for its display text, and the result

16
00:01:12,371 --> 00:01:14,747
lists all eight published posts.

17
00:01:15,205 --> 00:01:17,429
Add the where argument to filter.

18
00:01:17,429 --> 00:01:20,736
A condition is a field name plus an operator suffix:

19
00:01:20,736 --> 00:01:25,012
here the display text field with the contains suffix, set to the word

20
00:01:25,012 --> 00:01:27,065
with, which leaves two titles.

21
00:01:27,636 --> 00:01:31,545
A part that provides a filter adds it to where, either as an

22
00:01:31,545 --> 00:01:34,862
object named after the part or directly as fields.

23
00:01:34,862 --> 00:01:40,133
Autoroute adds its fields directly, so path starts with tutorials returns the three

24
00:01:40,133 --> 00:01:42,976
tutorials, and the result shows each path.

25
00:01:43,434 --> 00:01:49,539
Conditions combine with OR, AND and NOT, written in capitals, each taking a list.

26
00:01:49,539 --> 00:01:55,504
This OR keeps the posts whose display text contains Liquid or contains GraphQL.

27
00:01:59,704 --> 00:02:03,822
Each field has an equality condition and a not version.

28
00:02:03,822 --> 00:02:09,426
Strings add contains, starts with, ends with and in, each with a not version.

29
00:02:09,426 --> 00:02:14,153
Identifiers add in and not in, and dates add in, not in, greater

30
00:02:14,153 --> 00:02:17,394
than and less than, with or without equal.

31
00:02:18,144 --> 00:02:23,700
Several conditions side by side in the where argument must all hold, as with AND.

32
00:02:23,700 --> 00:02:29,383
OR needs at least one condition to hold, and NOT negates the condition it contains.

33
00:02:29,383 --> 00:02:31,874
AND, OR and NOT each take a list.

34
00:02:32,624 --> 00:02:35,885
Fields of your own parts can be filtered too.

35
00:02:35,885 --> 00:02:40,936
Boolean, date, date and time, numeric, text and time fields work when the

36
00:02:40,936 --> 00:02:46,564
Content Fields Indexing (SQL) feature is enabled, with the suffixes of their type.

37
00:02:50,863 --> 00:02:53,999
Without orderBy, items come newest first.

38
00:02:53,999 --> 00:02:59,803
Add orderBy with a field and a direction, ASC or DESC, and the result follows it:

39
00:02:59,803 --> 00:03:03,873
display text ascending lists the titles alphabetically.

40
00:03:04,329 --> 00:03:07,599
Creation date ascending puts the oldest post first.

41
00:03:07,599 --> 00:03:11,786
You can sort by the properties of the content item, such as display

42
00:03:11,786 --> 00:03:16,949
text and the created, modified and published dates, but not by the fields of a part.

43
00:03:21,247 --> 00:03:24,467
The first argument limits the number of items.

44
00:03:24,467 --> 00:03:28,927
With first set to three, the query returns the three newest posts.

45
00:03:29,383 --> 00:03:32,596
The skip argument leaves out items from the start.

46
00:03:32,596 --> 00:03:36,671
First three with skip three returns the next page, so skip is the

47
00:03:36,671 --> 00:03:39,483
page number minus one, times the page size.

48
00:03:39,934 --> 00:03:43,966
Skip six leaves two posts, so the last page is shorter.

49
00:03:43,966 --> 00:03:49,584
When first is missing or zero, the query returns the default number of results.

50
00:03:53,832 --> 00:03:57,246
A content item can have a published version and a draft.

51
00:03:57,246 --> 00:04:02,257
The status argument defaults to PUBLISHED, and DRAFT returns the most recent versions

52
00:04:02,257 --> 00:04:05,452
that are not published, here the Roadmap notes post.

53
00:04:05,968 --> 00:04:10,869
LATEST returns the most recent version of every item, published or draft, so

54
00:04:10,869 --> 00:04:13,738
the draft joins the eight published posts.

55
00:04:13,738 --> 00:04:16,308
ALL returns every historical version.

56
00:04:20,557 --> 00:04:22,529
The arguments work together.

57
00:04:22,529 --> 00:04:27,052
This query keeps the posts under guides, sorts them by display text, and

58
00:04:27,052 --> 00:04:29,198
returns the second page of two:

59
00:04:29,198 --> 00:04:32,387
Headless with GraphQL and Searching your content.

60
00:04:36,587 --> 00:04:40,882
Three settings in the GraphQL configuration section control paging.

61
00:04:40,882 --> 00:04:47,472
DefaultNumberOfResults applies when first is missing, MaxNumberOfResults is the largest first allowed, and

62
00:04:47,472 --> 00:04:51,532
MaxNumberOfResultsValidationMode decides what happens above it.

63
00:04:51,532 --> 00:04:54,297
They can also be set for a single tenant.

64
00:04:55,047 --> 00:04:57,231
The defaults are 100 and 1,000.

65
00:04:57,231 --> 00:05:02,366
Enabled fails validation when first is above the maximum, while Disabled logs the

66
00:05:02,366 --> 00:05:04,727
value without rejecting the query.

67
00:05:04,727 --> 00:05:09,567
Default means Enabled in the Development environment and Disabled elsewhere.

68
00:05:11,261 --> 00:05:13,235
Here the mode is Enabled.

69
00:05:13,235 --> 00:05:18,456
Asking for 1001 posts returns a validation error that names the argument and

70
00:05:18,456 --> 00:05:20,811
the maximum, 1000, and no data.

71
00:05:22,811 --> 00:05:28,116
Filters, ordering, paging and versions are arguments on every content type query.

72
00:05:28,116 --> 00:05:29,701
Thanks for watching.

